WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.166
Local LLM workloads vary widely.

2
00:00:02.166 --> 00:00:06.654
Interactive chat is latency-sensitive because a person is waiting.

3
00:00:06.654 --> 00:00:13.153
Coding agents may issue many sequential calls while reading files, proposing edits, and retrying.

4
00:00:13.153 --> 00:00:17.717
Batch summarization can tolerate longer waits but may create bursts.

5
00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:20.657
Document analysis stresses context length.

6
00:00:20.657 --> 00:00:24.912
Embedding jobs may use different models and endpoints entirely.

7
00:00:24.912 --> 00:00:29.864
Short requests and long-context requests create different routing needs.

8
00:00:29.864 --> 00:00:32.262
A quick prompt may finish in seconds.

9
00:00:32.262 --> 00:00:38.761
A large document prompt may spend significant time in prompt processing before generation begins.

10
00:00:38.761 --> 00:00:48.819
If those requests are treated as identical, one backend can become clogged with long jobs while another could have served short interactive work quickly.

11
00:00:48.819 --> 00:00:53.384
Concurrency turns simple user behavior into real capacity pressure.

12
00:00:53.384 --> 00:01:03.906
Several people may each think they are making one request, while the service sees overlapping prompt processing, token generation, and streaming connections.

13
00:01:03.906 --> 00:01:08.780
A coding assistant or tool-using agent may keep a backend busy for minutes.

14
00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:15.588
Routing should consider how much work each server can accept before queueing or rejecting new requests.

15
00:01:15.588 --> 00:01:18.528
Session continuity and retries also matter.

16
00:01:18.528 --> 00:01:24.718
Stateless clients send full context with each request and can route to any compatible backend.

17
00:01:24.718 --> 00:01:31.449
Other workflows rely on cache behavior, temporary state, or tool context and may need sticky sessions.

18
00:01:31.449 --> 00:01:40.578
Retries can improve reliability after a backend failure, but blind retries are risky when a request triggered actions or external tool use.
